WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.480
Speaker 1: The big economic news yesterday,&nbsp;
GDP down for the second quarter in a row.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.280
Many of the haters said, David, you will never&nbsp;
cover this because you have to defend the Joe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.834
Biden economy to the death.
Speaker 2: And I.&nbsp;

00:00:13.834 --> 00:00:19.920
Speaker 1: Know, guys, I don't care. I don't&nbsp;
care about defending Joe Biden. I don't care&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:24.880
about Democrats. I'm not a Democrat. I'm just a&nbsp;
progressive. I report to you what is not what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:34.080
would like to see or what someone else says should&nbsp;
be. GDP fell 0.9% in the second quarter of 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:40.320
This is the second straight decline and it is&nbsp;
being called a strong recession signal. There is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:48.480
a traditional delineation of what is a recession,&nbsp;
which is two consecutive quarters of declining GDP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:58.560
and indeed on an annualized basis, GDP fell 0.9%.&nbsp;
That's following a 1.6% decline in the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:05.680
quarter. And this was worse than the Dow Jones&nbsp;
estimate of a gain of 0.3%. The drop came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:12.640
a broad swath of factors, including decreases&nbsp;
in inventories, residential and nonresidential&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:18.480
investment and government spending. Now, I am&nbsp;
terrible at this. I believe humans are terrible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:25.040
at this predicting economic futures. I don't&nbsp;
believe we are headed to a recession. I believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.040
we are going to have better inflation numbers in&nbsp;
a couple of weeks. In mid-August, we're going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.440
get the July numbers. I believe they're going to&nbsp;
look good. I think there will be a nice little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.480 --> 00:01:39.920
potentially a little, little market rally there.&nbsp;
It's not actionable. I'm not doing anything. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:43.680
not buying, selling, suggesting any one of&nbsp;
these is just a prediction that I'm making.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:49.840
I think Q3 is going to end up being so-so and Q4&nbsp;
is going to be better. Now, that prediction is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:53.840
worth what you paid for it, which is nothing.&nbsp;
Okay, but this is my guess, and I'm going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:59.600
tell you a couple of different things. Why? First&nbsp;
and foremost, there is this conventional wisdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:07.200
which is often not that wise or conventional,&nbsp;
which is the American economy, by definition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:14.160
has a recession every 6 to 7 years at the most,&nbsp;
every ten. We are overdue for a recession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:22.240
I don't tend to believe that that is hard&nbsp;
and fast. It's possible, but difficult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:29.520
to avoid recessions, but it requires a very&nbsp;
balanced stimulation of supply and demand.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:34.800
And you can, in some theoretical&nbsp;
sense, do this via fiscal stimulus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.520
low interest rates, preventing shocks like&nbsp;
the 2008 financial crisis due to mortgage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:45.120
products that were predatory and fed into&nbsp;
the feedback loop. And now it never happens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.440
right? Like historically it just hasn't happened.&nbsp;
So we've had recessions on this regular basis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:55.200
I don't actually believe we have to have true&nbsp;
recessions, but we are certainly going to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.200 --> 00:03:00.640
pullbacks and we've been experiencing a pullback.&nbsp;
I am right now partial to the idea that we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:07.200
getting to the end of the pullback rather than&nbsp;
only the beginning. If you look at the reasons why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:15.120
the decrease in GDP took place, they're not the&nbsp;
most concerning reasons. I'll give you an example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:20.640
Reduction in inventories. Why would their&nbsp;
reduction in inventories lowers GDP?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:27.600
Why would you have a reduction in inventories?&nbsp;
Well, one reason can be that people are buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:34.640
everything. When people are buying everything and&nbsp;
consumer demand is so strong, it's not exactly a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:40.880
negative sign that inventories have declined even&nbsp;
though it lowers GDP. That's just one example.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.880
Second, there has been reduced federal government&nbsp;
spending. This is usually what right wingers want.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.680
When there is government spending, the&nbsp;
right wingers go, we can't afford it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:58.640
Inflation, it's bad. It's all terrible. Well, you&nbsp;
know what happens when the government spends less,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:04.960
the GDP goes down. That's another reason that GDP&nbsp;
is down, but it's not actually representative of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:08.720
private industry struggling. And again,&nbsp;
private industries is doing very well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.480
Residential fixed investment has declined.&nbsp;
Nonresidential fixed investment like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:23.360
these are not the GDP decreasing factors that to&nbsp;
me signal that a recession is coming. Now, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:28.560
I'm an idiot. I don't know anything. And I&nbsp;
think even the people who do know stuff are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.240
terrible at making predictions, but it's just not&nbsp;
feeling when you look at demand is really high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:41.760
inflation is coming down. We are close to full&nbsp;
employment. Tons of businesses have open jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:48.800
that they want to hire for. This doesn't. Feel.&nbsp;
Oh, my God. Am I saying feel? Do facts care about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.800 --> 00:04:54.240
my feelings? No, they don't. This doesn't feel&nbsp;
like we're heading into a recession. And I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.680
think that the next six months are going to be&nbsp;
disastrous. Now, would I take that to the bank?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:05.440
Hell, no. What am I doing? I'm doing the same&nbsp;
thing I always do. I've got my asset allocation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:14.400
It's, you know, 15% cash, 3% crypto. I've got&nbsp;
my real estate, I've got my stocks and bonds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:19.920
and I just invest monthly regardless of&nbsp;
what the market is doing. I'm not I don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.240
even trust myself enough in these predictions to&nbsp;
go and make any changes to what I'm doing. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:34.080
that's my take on this, and I know that.&nbsp;
And by the way, I don't credit Joe Biden for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.080 --> 00:05:40.560
any of it. I would be it. Listen. I was right&nbsp;
there when Barack Obama remember Barack Obama.&nbsp;

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.760
Speaker 2: Obama.
Speaker 1: When he was running for reelection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:50.160
and Mitt Romney was running against him. I said,&nbsp;
I think the economy will be good the next four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.360
years. Whether it's Romney or Obama, it might be&nbsp;
a little different, but it's basically going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.360 --> 00:06:00.800
be a strong economy and it just was going to be&nbsp;
a strong economy. If it was. If it was Romney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:05.280
then I would have had to say the same thing. It&nbsp;
was pretty strong. During a period under Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:09.600
the economy was fine. I would even say I wasn't&nbsp;
on here saying, Oh, Trump's economy is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:13.840
terrible. It was fine. Job growth slowed down&nbsp;
a little bit than where it was under Obama,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:19.440
but stock market was good, consumer confidence was&nbsp;
good, inflation was low. I don't I don't do the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:24.480
political games that many people do around this&nbsp;
stuff. And right now, my sense is this is not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:30.000
start of some massive recession. Let's see, you&nbsp;
know, four months from now, six months from now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.920
seven or eight months from now, come back&nbsp;
and check with me and we'll see where we are.

